/P 4293 0 R
/E (Add a little bit oFear \(of Losing Money\): On the flip side, fear can lead to hesitation in executing trades. Fearful traders may hold on to losing positions for too long, hoping the price will rebound, or they might avoid taking necessary risks, missing out on profitable opportunities.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 166 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
2523 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 4294 0 R
/E (Greed: Greed can be equally destructive. It can cause traders to risk too much on a single trade or fail to take profits at an appropriate level, holding out for higher returns and ending up with nothing. Greed often leads traders to over-leverage their positions, chasing after quick profits without regard for risk management.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 166 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2524 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 4295 0 R
/E (Overconfidence: Overconfidence is another common trap. A trader who has had a few successful trades may start to believe they are invincible, which can lead to bigger risks and eventual downfall. The crypto market is unpredictable, and even experienced traders can make mistakes.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 166 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2525 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 4296 0 R
/E (Loss Aversion: This cognitive bias makes traders more sensitive to losses than to equivalent gains. For example, a trader may hold onto a losing position, hoping for a bounce, rather than cutting the loss and moving on. This often results in greater losses, as the trader fails to manage risk properly.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 167 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
