2993 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (How to Use the Risk-to-Reward Ratio Identify entry and exit points: Determine where you plan to enter and exit your trade before you enter the market. This includes setting your stop-loss \(for risk\) and target price \(for reward\). Calculate the R/R ratio: If you are risking $100 on a trade and your potential profit is $300, then the risk-to-reward ratio is 1:3. Evaluate the trade: If the R/R ratio is less than 1:2, reconsider the trade. A lower R/R ratio means that the trade may not be worth the risk.)
/E (How to Use the Risk-to-Reward Ratio Identify entry and exit points: Determine where you plan to enter and exit your trade before you enter the market. This includes setting your stop-loss \(for risk\) and target price \(for reward\). Calculate the R/R ratio: If you are risking $100 on a trade and your potential profit is $300, then the risk-to-reward ratio is 1:3. Evaluate the trade: If the R/R ratio is less than 1:2, reconsider the trade. A lower R/R ratio means that the trade may not be worth the risk.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 273 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2994 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Why the Risk-to-Reward Ratio Matters Having a solid R/R ratio ensures that your trading strategy remains profitable over time, even if you experience a series of losses. By ensuring that you make more on winning trades than you lose on losing trades, you can remain in the market long enough to come out ahead in the long run.)
/E (Why the Risk-to-Reward Ratio Matters Having a solid R/R ratio ensures that your trading strategy remains profitable over time, even if you experience a series of losses. By ensuring that you make more on winning trades than you lose on losing trades, you can remain in the market long enough to come out ahead in the long run.)
